            <ArticleTitle>Iontophoretically applied lidocaine reduces pain on propofol injection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>432-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We have compared iontophoretically and locally applied lidocaine for relief of pain on propofol injection. Pain was assessed on insertion of a 20-gauge i.v. cannula and at 10-s intervals for 30 s after injection of propofol. Pain scores on cannulation were significantly less in the iontophoresis group (median 1.1) than in the sham (control) group (median 2.8) (P &lt; 0.005). Pain after injection of propofol was significantly reduced at 10 (P &lt; 0.002), 20 (P &lt; 0.001) and 30 s (P &lt; 0.001). We conclude that iontophoretically applied lidocaine decreased the pain of cannulation and propofol injection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
